1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
In the last episode, we saw that a

2
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:07,200
mixture of experts model runs only a few

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:11,320
of its experts for any single token. In

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:13,320
Deep Seek V3's case, eight out of 256,

5
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:14,360
while the full set stays resident in

6
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:16,680
memory.

7
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:18,240
A design like that only works when

8
00:00:16,680 --> 00:00:20,480
enough requests are flowing through the

9
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:22,400
machine at the same moment to keep every

10
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:24,480
expert busy.

11
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:26,920
This episode is about the layer whose

12
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:29,560
job is to create that condition.

13
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:32,200
The serving system, which takes a fixed

14
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:35,280
set of GPUs running a trillion parameter

15
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:39,040
model and serves as many users as it can

16
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:41,520
without missing anyone's latency target.

17
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:44,640
The place to start is the worst case,

18
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:46,920
one GPU serving one request.

19
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,120
To generate a single token, the GPU

20
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:51,600
reads every weight the token touches out

21
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:53,880
of high-bandwidth memory, runs a thin

22
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,680
matrix vector product against them, and

23
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:58,400
writes the token back. The tensor cores

24
00:00:56,680 --> 00:01:01,000
barely warm up.

25
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:03,440
Arithmetic intensity, the useful work

26
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,560
done per byte moved, sits far to the

27
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:07,680
left of the roofline's ridge, and the

28
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:11,600
chip spends nearly all of its time

29
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:14,120
waiting on memory rather than computing.

30
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:16,680
And nothing about that expensive read

31
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:18,800
was specific to the request. The same

32
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:21,560
full pass over the weights would have

33
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:23,560
happened whether one user was connected

34
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:25,480
or 64.

35
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:27,080
That observation raises the question

36
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,320
that we'll try to answer in this

37
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:30,440
episode.

38
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:33,160
How many tokens does the system get out

39
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:35,520
of each full read of the weights?

40
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:38,080
Serving one request at a time, it gets

41
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:40,120
exactly one, and nearly every technique

42
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:42,080
we're about to see is a way of raising

43
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:44,760
that number.

44
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:47,000
Batching is how a serving system raises

45
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:49,800
that number, and it works for one

46
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,880
reason. The weights are identical for

47
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:54,400
every request.

48
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:56,760
When the GPU pulls a weight matrix out

49
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:58,760
of high-bandwidth memory, nothing about

50
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:59,880
that matrix belongs to any particular

51
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:01,520
user.

52
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:04,120
So, instead of reading the weights,

53
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:06,680
computing one token, and starting over,

54
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:09,360
the system gathers N requests that each

55
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:10,920
need a decode step, reads each weight

56
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:13,480
matrix once,

57
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:15,959
and multiplies it against all N token

58
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:18,680
vectors at the same time.

59
00:02:15,959 --> 00:02:21,959
The thin matrix vector product becomes a

60
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:23,160
fat matrix matrix product.

61
00:02:21,959 --> 00:02:24,760
Look at what that does to the

62
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:27,520
arithmetic.

63
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:29,800
The bytes moved stay almost flat because

64
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:31,560
the weights are still read only once,

65
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,360
while the useful floating-point work

66
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:35,480
grows with N.

67
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:38,000
Arithmetic intensity rises by roughly a

68
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:40,200
factor of N, and decode walks up the

69
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,280
roofline, away from the memory-bound

70
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,520
floor, and toward the compute-bound

71
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:46,280
region where the tensor cores finally

72
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:48,560
run hot.

73
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:50,959
One read of the weights now produces N

74
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:53,600
tokens instead of one.

75
00:02:50,959 --> 00:02:55,959
Arithmetic intensity is what decides

76
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:58,400
whether those cores are used at all, and

77
00:02:55,959 --> 00:03:00,080
batch size is what moves it,

78
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,040
which is why most of what follows in

79
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:04,440
this episode is about keeping the batch

80
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:07,320
full, or about repairing a problem that

81
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:09,920
keeping it full creates.

82
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:11,400
That N, though, is not a number anyone

83
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:13,680
sets.

84
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:16,160
It is however many requests happen to be

85
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:17,760
generating tokens at that particular

86
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:20,040
step.

87
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:23,239
In the first serving systems, it was

88
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:25,440
fixed the moment the batch was formed.

89
00:03:23,239 --> 00:03:28,160
They scheduled at the granularity of a

90
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:29,440
whole request, collecting a batch,

91
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:32,280
running it,

92
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:34,880
and holding its composition unchanged

93
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:37,440
until every sequence had emitted an

94
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:39,760
end-of-sequence token.

95
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:44,920
Take four requests that arrive together

96
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:46,480
and happen to need 8, 24, 60, and 512

97
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:49,640
decode steps.

98
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:51,080
The batch is locked for 512 steps

99
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:53,720
because that is when the last one

100
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:55,960
finishes. The first request is done

101
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:59,400
after eight, and its slot keeps running

102
00:03:55,960 --> 00:04:01,080
for the remaining 504.

103
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:03,720
Add up the work the batch actually

104
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:05,800
delivers, and it comes to under a third

105
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:07,920
of what it had room for.

106
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:11,280
With a single request active for the

107
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:14,320
final 452 steps, while three slots

108
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:16,320
compute nothing anyone asked for.

109
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:18,840
And a finished request cannot even be

110
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:20,959
returned to its user until the whole

111
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:23,080
batch drains.

112
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:26,400
So, the count of requests doing useful

113
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:28,480
work falls step by step toward one,

114
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:29,720
while each iteration still reads every

115
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:31,520
weight.

116
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:34,400
The tokens coming out of each read of

117
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:36,200
the weights slide back toward one, and

118
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:39,680
they do it exactly when a long

119
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:42,120
generation is holding the batch open.

120
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:44,000
Suppose the batch does stay full. There

121
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:46,160
is still a ceiling on how large it can

122
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,320
get, and it comes from the one thing in

123
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,960
the forward pass that no two requests

124
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:52,040
share.

125
00:04:49,960 --> 00:04:54,800
The weights are identical for every

126
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:57,000
request, which is why a single read of

127
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:59,720
them serves the whole batch.

128
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,240
A KV cache is the opposite.

129
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:04,560
Every request carries its own keys and

130
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:07,280
values, the running record of its own

131
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:10,440
sequence. So, attention has to read each

132
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:13,360
cache separately, one read per request

133
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:16,800
on every step. Those reads scale with

134
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:18,680
the batch, and none of them amortize.

135
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:20,360
So, attention stays on the memory bound

136
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:22,520
floor no matter how large the batch

137
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:25,120
grows. And while those caches are being

138
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:26,640
loaded, the arithmetic units sit nearly

139
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:29,200
idle.

140
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:31,280
The same caches decide the ceiling.

141
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,240
Model weights are a fixed cost in

142
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:35,480
memory, and activations take very

143
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:38,000
little. So, whatever high-bandwidth

144
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:40,000
memory is left over is what holds KV

145
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,040
caches, and that is what sets the

146
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,200
maximum batch size.

147
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:46,760
Hand every spare byte on the card to KV

148
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:49,360
cache, and you still fit only a few tens

149
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:52,600
of concurrent requests. You run out of

150
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:55,120
room before you run out of arithmetic.

151
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:57,720
That is what makes paged attention from

152
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:59,920
episode 3 matter here.

153
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:02,160
The systems before it pre-allocated a

154
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:04,800
contiguous block of memory for each

155
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:07,160
request sized for the longest output

156
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:10,440
that request might ever produce.

157
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:13,160
And profiling found that only 20 to 38%

158
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:14,800
of it ever held real token data.

159
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:16,240
The rest was reservation and

160
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:18,840
fragmentation.

161
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:21,640
Storing the cache in non-contiguous

162
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:24,640
fixed-size blocks, the way an operating

163
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:27,400
system pages memory, brings that waste

164
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:30,240
near zero. So, more sequences fit on the

165
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:32,200
same card, the batch grows,

166
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:34,040
and each read of the weights covers more

167
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:36,200
tokens.

168
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:38,520
Capacity sets how large the batch can

169
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:40,640
be. Keeping it that large is a

170
00:06:38,520 --> 00:06:42,960
scheduling problem. And the static

171
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:45,240
approach fails it by freezing the batch

172
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:49,560
the moment it forms.

173
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:52,200
Orca, presented at OSDI in 2022, changed

174
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:53,640
what the scheduler hands to the engine.

175
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:56,400
Rather than a batch to run to

176
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:58,919
completion, it hands over a batch and

177
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:01,960
asks for exactly one iteration, one

178
00:06:58,919 --> 00:07:04,720
token for every active request.

179
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:06,800
The engine runs that step and returns.

180
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,240
Between steps, the scheduler drops

181
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:10,600
whatever just emitted [music] an end of

182
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:12,600
sequence token, admits whatever has been

183
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:13,919
waiting, and rebuilds the batch for the

184
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,840
next step.

185
00:07:13,919 --> 00:07:18,120
Orca called this iteration-level

186
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:20,160
scheduling. It is now usually called

187
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:22,600
continuous batching.

188
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:25,040
It works because one iteration is the

189
00:07:22,600 --> 00:07:26,400
same small unit of work for every

190
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:29,080
request.

191
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:31,960
Whether a sequence is on its fifth token

192
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:34,240
or its 500th, a step advances it by

193
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:36,840
exactly one. So, the batch can be

194
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:39,040
rebuilt between any two steps without

195
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:41,120
interrupting anyone.

196
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:43,600
Take the same four requests. The

197
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:45,880
eight-step one now leaves at step eight,

198
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:48,960
and a waiting request takes its seat,

199
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:51,920
and the same happens at 24 and at 60.

200
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:53,840
No seat sits idle for hundreds of steps,

201
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:56,640
and the batch stays full instead of

202
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:59,280
draining to a single survivor.

203
00:07:56,640 --> 00:08:02,120
Against Nvidia's FasterTransformer on

204
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:05,200
GPT-3 175B,

205
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:08,120
holding a median latency of 190

206
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:10,800
milliseconds per generated token,

207
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:12,120
Orca served 6.81

208
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:15,680
requests per second, where

209
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:18,800
FasterTransformer served 0.185,

210
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:20,480
a factor of 36.9.

211
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:23,240
VLLM,

212
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:26,720
measured against Orca a year later,

213
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:28,600
reported two to four times.

214
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:30,800
But those requests now sit at different

215
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:32,479
positions in their sequences, so the

216
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:34,880
tensors flowing through attention have

217
00:08:32,479 --> 00:08:37,520
different shapes, and Orca's paper is

218
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:39,520
explicit that for any two of them,

219
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:41,320
there is no guarantee the next iteration

220
00:08:39,520 --> 00:08:43,280
can be merged into one batched

221
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:45,440
operation.

222
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:47,680
Orca's second contribution is the

223
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:50,320
observation that this problem does not

224
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:52,720
apply to every operation.

225
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:54,760
Most of a transformer layer treats each

226
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:57,560
token on its own.

227
00:08:54,760 --> 00:09:00,000
The linear projections, the layer norms,

228
00:08:57,560 --> 00:09:02,240
the activation function, none of them

229
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,240
need to know which request a token came

230
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:05,000
from.

231
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:07,440
So the engine stops organizing by

232
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,440
request and stacks every token from

233
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:12,960
every request into one flat tensor.

234
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:15,680
In the paper's example, four requests

235
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:17,280
contribute seven tokens between them,

236
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:19,880
and the layers' weight matrix

237
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:21,839
multiplications run once across those

238
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:24,040
seven rows.

239
00:09:21,839 --> 00:09:26,160
Attention is the exception because it is

240
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:27,240
the operation that mixes tokens with one

241
00:09:26,160 --> 00:09:29,520
another.

242
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:32,080
A token may attend only to tokens from

243
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:34,320
its own request, so attention has to

244
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:36,640
respect where each request begins and

245
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:39,560
ends, and each request carries a

246
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:42,400
different length of history.

247
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:44,640
Selective batching does both at once.

248
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:46,920
It batches every other operation across

249
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:50,360
all the tokens together and splits

250
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:52,781
attention out to run request by request.

251
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:53,080
Splitting it out costs almost nothing.

252
00:09:52,781 --> 00:09:54,680
>> [music]

253
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:56,480
>> And the reason reaches back to why

254
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:58,480
batching worked at all.

255
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:01,120
Batching pays by making one read of the

256
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:03,600
weights serve many requests.

257
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:05,720
Attention has no weights of its own,

258
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:08,520
only keys and values belonging to

259
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:11,080
individual requests. So, there was never

260
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:13,080
anything there to share.

261
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:14,960
There is one kind of work in that batch,

262
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:17,600
unlike the rest.

263
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:20,320
A decode step advances a request by a

264
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:22,880
single token. But, a request that has

265
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:25,480
just been admitted has to process its

266
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:27,760
whole prompt first and prompts run to

267
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:29,920
thousands of tokens.

268
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:32,440
Both are one iteration.

269
00:10:29,920 --> 00:10:34,720
So, the moment a new request joins, that

270
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:37,360
iteration stops being a step and becomes

271
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:39,760
a bulk job with everyone else's next

272
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:42,280
token sitting behind it. Against a

273
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:44,200
decode-only batch, mixing them naively

274
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:47,000
stretched the time between tokens by as

275
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:48,880
much as 28 times. And in vLLM, it

276
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:50,520
produced generation stalls lasting

277
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:53,360
several seconds.

278
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:54,960
Where users, mid-answer, simply stop

279
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:57,800
receiving text [music] while a

280
00:10:54,960 --> 00:11:01,040
stranger's prompt is processed.

281
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:03,720
Sarathi, a 2023 paper from Microsoft

