00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:06,440
Research India and Georgia Tech, splits

283
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:08,920
the prefill into near-equal chunks and

284
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:10,800
its follow-up, Sarathi Serve, builds a

285
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:13,360
scheduler around them.

286
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:14,600
Each iteration gets a fixed token

287
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:16,760
budget.

288
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:19,040
The running decodes are packed in first,

289
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:20,680
one token each, and whatever is left of

290
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:22,560
the budget is filled with a chunk of a

291
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:24,920
waiting prompt.

292
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:27,360
That bounds the cost of an iteration and

293
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:29,280
makes it nearly independent of how long

294
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:31,760
the incoming prompt is.

295
00:11:29,280 --> 00:11:34,200
Admitting new work no longer pauses

296
00:11:31,760 --> 00:11:36,400
generation already in flight, which is

297
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:38,080
what the authors call a stall-free

298
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:40,760
schedule.

299
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:43,400
Chunk size is a real trade.

300
00:11:40,760 --> 00:11:45,520
Small chunks keep each iteration short

301
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:48,120
and the token stream smooth, but the

302
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:50,720
prompt needs more iterations to finish,

303
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:53,400
so its own first token arrives later,

304
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:55,680
and every chunk rereads [music] the KV

305
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:58,600
cache the earlier ones wrote.

306
00:11:55,680 --> 00:12:01,080
Large chunks reverse both.

307
00:11:58,600 --> 00:12:03,920
Under tail latency limits, Sarathi serve

308
00:12:01,080 --> 00:12:07,960
reported 2.6 times the serving capacity

309
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:12,200
of vLLM for Mistral 7B on 1 A100, and up

310
00:12:07,960 --> 00:12:14,200
to 3.7 times for E34B on 2.

311
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:15,800
With pipeline parallelism on Falcon

312
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:18,720
180B,

313
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:21,000
the gain reached up to 5.6 times, helped

314
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:23,400
by a second effect, iterations of

315
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,120
uniform size leave fewer pipeline

316
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:27,680
bubbles.

317
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:29,640
vLLM now enables chunked prefill by

318
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:32,520
default.

319
00:12:29,640 --> 00:12:35,400
Chunked prefill lets prefill and decode

320
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:38,000
share one GPU without the stalls, but

321
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:40,160
they remain opposite workloads.

322
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:42,440
Prefill processes thousands of prompt

323
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:44,480
tokens at once and saturates the

324
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:47,240
arithmetic units.

325
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:49,400
Decode produces one token per request

326
00:12:47,240 --> 00:12:51,960
and leaves those same units starved,

327
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:54,480
held back by memory bandwidth.

328
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:56,920
One machine means one hardware choice

329
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:59,520
and one parallelism strategy for both,

330
00:12:56,920 --> 00:13:02,320
which compromises each.

331
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:05,080
Disaggregation gives each its own.

332
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:07,320
Prefill runs on one pool of GPUs, decode

333
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:09,800
on another, each configured for its own

334
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:11,760
bottleneck, and the KV cache the prefill

335
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:14,680
pool builds is shipped across the

336
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:17,080
interconnect to the decode pool. [music]

337
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:18,680
That transfer is the price, and disserve

338
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:23,160
put a number on it.

339
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:27,120
A single 512 token request on OPT-66B

340
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:29,360
produces about 1.13 GB of KV cache, so

341
00:13:27,120 --> 00:13:32,400
at 10 requests a second, you need

342
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:34,920
roughly 90 GB per second of network just

343
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:37,360
to keep the transfer invisible.

344
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:39,000
A cluster with InfiniBand or NVLink

345
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:41,480
absorbs that.

346
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:43,960
A small deployment does not, which makes

347
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:47,280
disaggregation a decision about how much

348
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:49,320
traffic you have, not a default.

349
00:13:47,280 --> 00:13:51,400
DisServe and SplitWise were concurrent

350
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:53,320
independent research work arriving at

351
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:56,920
the same conclusion.

352
00:13:53,320 --> 00:14:00,160
DisServe reported serving 7.4 times more

353
00:13:56,920 --> 00:14:02,880
requests or meeting targets 12.6 times

354
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:05,560
tighter while holding over 90% of

355
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:07,600
requests inside both their first token

356
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:10,760
and per token budgets

357
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:12,400
on models from 13 to 175 billion

358
00:14:10,760 --> 00:14:14,520
parameters.

359
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:17,960
SplitWise reported 1.4 times the

360
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:21,240
throughput at 20% lower cost or 2.35

361
00:14:17,960 --> 00:14:23,360
times at the same cost and power.

362
00:14:21,240 --> 00:14:24,960
Production pushed it further, and here

363
00:14:23,360 --> 00:14:26,680
we go back to what we covered in the

364
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:29,120
last episode.

365
00:14:26,680 --> 00:14:31,720
DeepSeek runs its prefill pool at 32-way

366
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:34,000
expert parallelism and its decode pool

367
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:36,560
at 144-way.

368
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:39,920
The reason is sparsity. With eight of

369
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:42,080
256 experts firing per token, only an

370
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:43,800
enormous combined batch gives every

371
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:44,260
expert enough tokens to be worth

372
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:44,400
running,

373
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:46,200
>> [music]

374
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:47,920
>> and decode is where that batch gets

375
00:14:46,200 --> 00:14:50,560
assembled.

376
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:52,600
MoonCake, the platform behind Kimi,

377
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:54,400
built its whole architecture around the

378
00:14:52,600 --> 00:14:58,160
KV cache.

379
00:14:54,400 --> 00:15:00,680
And by 2025, the pattern was standard.

380
00:14:58,160 --> 00:15:04,320
Nvidia Dynamo, the Kubernetes native

381
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:08,560
LLMD, and SG Lang's reproduction on 96

382
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:12,600
H100s, which sustained 52.3K input and

383
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:14,880
22.3K output tokens per second per node

384
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:16,880
at about 20 cents per million output

385
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:18,800
tokens.

386
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:21,120
Splitting the pools made each phase

387
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:22,880
efficient, but it did not reduce the

388
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:25,080
amount of work.

389
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:27,960
The pre-filled pool still computes every

390
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:31,520
prompt from scratch, even when requests

391
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:33,080
share long stretches of identical text.

392
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:36,280
In production, that overlap is the

393
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:38,840
normal case, rather than the exception.

394
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:41,440
A system prompt sent on every call, the

395
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:44,240
same few-shot examples, a retrieved

396
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:46,040
document reused across many questions,

397
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:47,080
the growing transcript of a multi-turn

398
00:15:46,040 --> 00:15:49,240
chat,

399
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:51,440
an agent resending its entire context on

400
00:15:49,240 --> 00:15:53,600
every step, etc.

401
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:55,120
So, compute the shared part once and

402
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:57,920
reuse it.

403
00:15:55,120 --> 00:16:00,320
That works because attention is causal.

404
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:02,440
A token's keys and values are determined

405
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:04,640
by the text up to and including that

406
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:06,120
token, and nothing that comes after

407
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:08,240
changes them.

408
00:16:06,120 --> 00:16:11,200
So, the same prefix produces the same

409
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:13,360
keys and values every time.

410
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:15,600
A request arriving with that prefix

411
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:19,040
skips straight past the shared part of

412
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:21,280
its pre-fill to its first new token.

413
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:24,760
S G Lang's radix attention keeps them in

414
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:26,920
a radix tree keyed on token sequences.

415
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:29,400
A radix tree is a prefix tree whose

416
00:16:26,920 --> 00:16:31,720
edges can carry whole sequences, rather

417
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:34,800
than single tokens, which is what makes

418
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:36,960
it compact. Requests sharing a prefix

419
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:38,960
walk the same path through it, and the

420
00:16:36,960 --> 00:16:41,360
tree branches only at the token where

421
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:43,600
their sequences first differ.

422
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:46,560
When memory runs short, it evicts the

423
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:48,960
least recently used leaf, working inward

424
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:50,839
toward the shared trunk, and a reference

425
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:52,720
count on each node keeps it from

426
00:16:50,839 --> 00:16:54,560
evicting anything a running request is

427
00:16:52,720 --> 00:16:56,280
still using.

428
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:57,400
This works because the cache was already

429
00:16:56,280 --> 00:16:59,280
paged.

430
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:01,520
Sharing a prefix is two sequences

431
00:16:59,280 --> 00:17:03,280
pointing at the same physical blocks.

432
00:17:01,520 --> 00:17:05,360
Nothing is copied because those [music]

433
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:07,480
blocks can never change.

434
00:17:05,360 --> 00:17:10,199
Only the one partially filled block at

435
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:12,480
the point of divergence gets copied,

436
00:17:10,199 --> 00:17:15,959
exactly the copy-on-write an operating

437
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:18,120
system does when it forks a process.

438
00:17:15,959 --> 00:17:21,000
One consequence reaches up into the

439
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:23,360
cluster. A cached prefix lives in one

440
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,880
replica's memory, so a request only

441
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:28,480
benefits if it lands on that replica,

442
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:30,920
and routing has to become cache aware.

443
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:33,160
Send each request to the machine already

444
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:35,840
holding its prefix, not to whichever

445
00:17:33,160 --> 00:17:37,800
machine is least busy.

446
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:39,080
The pricing tells you what this is

447
00:17:37,800 --> 00:17:41,800
worth.

448
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:44,680
Anthropic and OpenAI both build a cached

449
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:45,800
prompt read at 1/10 of the normal input

450
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:47,760
price.

451
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:51,240
Anthropic charges a premium to write the

452
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:53,840
cache. 1.25 times normal for a 5-minute

453
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:56,120
lifetime, so a prefix used twice has

454
00:17:53,840 --> 00:17:58,680
already paid for itself.

455
00:17:56,120 --> 00:18:00,840
Agents lean on this hardest because an

456
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:04,320
agent replays its whole accumulated

457
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:06,440
history on every single step, and almost

458
00:18:04,320 --> 00:18:08,240
all of it is a prefix it has sent

459
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:10,560
before.

460
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:12,640
All of this lives in software, and that

461
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:15,080
software comes in two layers that get

462
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:16,960
conflated constantly.

463
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:19,480
An inference engine runs the model on

464
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:21,640
one GPU or one node. It owns the

465
00:18:19,480 --> 00:18:23,440
scheduler, the KV cache, and the

466
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:27,120
kernels.

467
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:31,200
VLLM, now a PyTorch Foundation project,

468
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:34,760
runs on Nvidia, AMD, Google TPUs, AWS

469
00:18:31,200 --> 00:18:37,840
Inferentia, Intel, and Apple Silicon.

470
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:39,960
SGLang is the engine behind the 96 GPU

471
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:41,600
Deep Seek reproduction.

472
00:18:39,960 --> 00:18:44,800
TensorRT-LLM

473
00:18:41,600 --> 00:18:46,280
is Nvidia's own tuned hardest for Nvidia

474
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:48,360
hardware.

475
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:51,120
And Hugging Face's text generation

476
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:53,760
inference is the serving toolkit built

477
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:56,040
around models from the hub.

478
00:18:53,760 --> 00:18:58,160
An orchestration layer sits above them

479
00:18:56,040 --> 00:19:00,280
and coordinates a fleet.

480
00:18:58,160 --> 00:19:02,920
Nvidia Dynamo and the Kubernetes native

481
00:19:00,280 --> 00:19:03,800
LLMD do the work no single engine can

482
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:06,200
see,

483
00:19:03,800 --> 00:19:08,920
placing prefill and decode pools across

484
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:11,800
nodes, routing each request to the

485
00:19:08,920 --> 00:19:15,240
replica that already holds its prefix,

486
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:17,600
and moving KV cache between machines.

487
00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:20,160
Neither replaces an engine.

488
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:23,080
Both run engines underneath, and that is

489
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:25,000
the important distinction.

490
00:19:23,080 --> 00:19:26,160
What all of it optimizes is not

491
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,640
throughput.

492
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:29,320
Throughput counts every token the

493
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:31,120
cluster produces.

494
00:19:29,320 --> 00:19:33,360
Goodput, the metric from the first

495
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:36,600
episode, counts only the ones that

496
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:38,880
arrived inside their latency targets.

497
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:41,400
Serving adds a per GPU version of the

498
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:44,440
same idea. The highest request rate a

499
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:46,320
single GPU can sustain while 90% of

500
00:19:44,440 --> 00:19:48,880
requests still meet both their first

501
00:19:46,320 --> 00:19:51,000
token and per token targets.

502
00:19:48,880 --> 00:19:53,360
A token generated for a request that

503
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:56,040
already blew its deadline is work you

504
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:58,720
paid for and cannot sell. So, a cluster

505
00:19:56,040 --> 00:20:00,040
can run at full utilization and deliver

506
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:02,760
very little.

507
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:04,480
And counted per GPU, goodput converts

508
00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:05,960
directly into the number the business

509
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:08,320
cares about.

510
00:20:05,960 --> 00:20:09,560
Cost per query.

511
00:20:08,320 --> 00:20:12,000
Defending goodput falls to the

512
00:20:09,560 --> 00:20:14,680
scheduler, and much of what it does is

513
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:17,520
decide which work to refuse.

514
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:19,440
Memory runs out first. KV caches grow as

515
00:20:17,520 --> 00:20:21,880
their sequences grow, so [music] a batch

516
00:20:19,440 --> 00:20:24,240
that fit a minute ago may not fit now,

517
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:27,200
and something has to be evicted.

518
00:20:24,240 --> 00:20:29,320
vLLM evicts a whole sequence at a time,

519
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:31,080
all of its blocks or none, because

520
00:20:29,320 --> 00:20:33,760
attention needs all of them together

521
00:20:31,080 --> 00:20:35,280
anyway. Getting that sequence back costs

522
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:37,480
one of two things.

523
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:40,520
Swapping copies its blocks out to CPU

524
00:20:37,480 --> 00:20:42,000
memory and later back again. Paid in bus

525
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:44,480
bandwidth.

526
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:47,640
Recomputing discards them and rebuilds

527
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:49,800
them later. Paid in arithmetic.

528
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:51,760
Recomputing is cheaper than it sounds,

529
00:20:49,800 --> 00:20:53,920
because the tokens the request already

530
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:56,200
generated can be appended to its

531
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:58,880
original prompt, and the whole cache

532
00:20:56,200 --> 00:21:01,160
rebuilt in a single prefill pass instead

533
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:03,920
of one token at a time.

534
00:21:01,160 --> 00:21:06,400
Which one wins depends on the machine.

535
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:10,560
How much bandwidth sits between CPU and

536
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:12,200
GPU against how much compute is spare.

537
00:21:10,560 --> 00:21:15,200
When one deployment serves many

538
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:17,880
customers, a single heavy user can crowd

539
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:20,400
out the rest and counting requests will

540
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:23,640
not catch it because one request can be

541
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:26,440
a hundred times the size of another.

542
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:28,960
The virtual token counter from a 2024

543
00:21:26,440 --> 00:21:31,360
fairness paper keeps a running total of

544
00:21:28,960 --> 00:21:33,760
the input and output tokens each tenant

545
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:36,120
has consumed and serves whoever has

546
00:21:33,760 --> 00:21:37,960
received the least.

547
00:21:36,120 --> 00:21:40,480
When more requests arrive than the

548
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:42,440
system can possibly finish in budget,

549
00:21:40,480 --> 00:21:45,080
accepting all of them spreads the

550
00:21:42,440 --> 00:21:48,160
machine so thin that everyone misses

551
00:21:45,080 --> 00:21:51,720
their deadline and good put goes to zero

552
00:21:48,160 --> 00:21:53,760
while the GPUs sit at full utilization.

553
00:21:51,720 --> 00:21:56,400
Admission control refuses the excess

554
00:21:53,760 --> 00:21:58,800
instead. Predict which requests cannot

555
00:21:56,400 --> 00:22:01,880
finish in time, then reject or defer

556
00:21:58,800 --> 00:22:04,880
them so the remainder still lands.

557
00:22:01,880 --> 00:22:08,640
Mooncake serving Kimi does this with a

558
00:22:04,880 --> 00:22:10,320
prediction-based early rejection policy.

559
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:13,600
And when the pools themselves are the

560
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:16,760
wrong shape, auto scaling corrects them.

561
00:22:13,600 --> 00:22:20,200
Dynamo's planner watches load across the

562
00:22:16,760 --> 00:22:22,480
prefill and decode pools and moves GPUs

563
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:24,760
from one to the other so the split

564
00:22:22,480 --> 00:22:26,960
tracks the traffic actually arriving

565
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:29,840
rather than the traffic someone assumed

566
00:22:26,960 --> 00:22:31,640
when the cluster was deployed.

567
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:32,680
The scheduler decides which requests

568
00:22:31,640 --> 00:22:34,720
run.

569
00:22:32,680 --> 00:22:37,840
What comes out of them is decided one

570
00:22:34,720 --> 00:22:40,720
stage later by the sampler.

571
00:22:37,840 --> 00:22:43,240
Every forward pass ends the same way.

572
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:45,360
A vector of logits, one raw score for

573
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:47,320
every token in the vocabulary, which

574
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:48,800
softmax turns into a probability

575
00:22:47,320 --> 00:22:50,840
distribution.

576
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:53,320
The sampler's job is to pick one token

577
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:55,640
from it and relative to a forward pass

578
00:22:53,320 --> 00:22:56,825
over a trillion parameters, that job is

579
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:57,080
nearly free.

580
00:22:56,825 --> 00:22:59,040
>> [music]

581
00:22:57,080 --> 00:23:01,320
>> It is also where every user-facing

582
00:22:59,040 --> 00:23:03,040
quality knob lives.

583
00:23:01,320 --> 00:23:05,600
The obvious approach is to take the

584
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:07,280
highest scoring token every time. It

585
00:23:05,600 --> 00:23:10,160
does not work.

586
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:13,000
Holtzman and colleagues showed in 2020

587
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:15,640
that maximization based decoding, greedy

588
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:18,240
or beam search, produces what they named

589
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:20,400
degeneration. Text that is bland,

590
00:23:18,240 --> 00:23:21,800
incoherent, or stuck in repetitive

591
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:24,760
loops.

592
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:27,000
In their example, beam search on GPT-2

593
00:23:24,760 --> 00:23:28,440
repeats the same university name over

594
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,800
and over.

595
00:23:28,440 --> 00:23:33,000
Models do assign high probability to

596
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:35,160
well-formed text, but the highest

597
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:37,840
scoring long continuations tend to be

598
00:23:35,160 --> 00:23:39,200
generic and repetitive.

599
00:23:37,840 --> 00:23:41,000
Sampling straight from the raw

600
00:23:39,200 --> 00:23:42,360
distribution fails in the opposite

601
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:45,360
direction.

602
00:23:42,360 --> 00:23:47,480
A language model's tail is unreliable.

603
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:49,560
Thousands of tokens, each holding a tiny

604
00:23:47,480 --> 00:23:51,720
share of the probability, and enough

605
00:23:49,560 --> 00:23:54,520
mass between them that incoherent

606
00:23:51,720 --> 00:23:56,800
choices get drawn regularly.

607
00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:59,240
So, the sampler does two things. It

608
00:23:56,800 --> 00:24:01,160
reshapes the distribution, and it cuts

609
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:03,480
off the tail.

610
00:24:01,160 --> 00:24:06,120
Temperature does the reshaping.

611
00:24:03,480 --> 00:24:07,566
Divide every logit by a temperature T

612
00:24:06,120 --> 00:24:07,840
before the softmax.

613
00:24:07,566 --> 00:24:10,680
>> [music]

614
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:13,320
>> At T of 1, nothing changes.

615
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:15,120
Below 1, the gaps between scores widen,

616
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:17,360
and the distribution sharpens toward a

617
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:18,480
spike on the highest score, approaching

618
00:24:17,360 --> 00:24:20,600
greedy.

619
00:24:18,480 --> 00:24:23,880
Above 1, it flattens, spreading

620
00:24:20,600 --> 00:24:25,880
probability toward unlikely tokens.

621
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:27,400
Truncation does the cutting in one of

622
00:24:25,880 --> 00:24:30,080
three ways.

623
00:24:27,400 --> 00:24:32,920
Top K keeps the K highest probability

624
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:36,280
tokens, a fixed short list that pays no

625
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:39,040
attention to how confident the model is.

626
00:24:36,280 --> 00:24:41,120
Top P, or nucleus sampling, keeps the

627
00:24:39,040 --> 00:24:43,600
smallest set whose probability is some

628
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:45,200
past P, which means it reads the shape

629
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:47,120
of the distribution.

630
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:49,640
When the model is confident, the set is

631
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:51,280
tiny, and when it is unsure, the set

632
00:24:49,640 --> 00:24:53,760
widens.

633
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:56,160
Min P sets its cut-off by scaling the

634
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:58,640
top token's probability, keeping only

635
00:24:56,160 --> 00:25:00,640
tokens at least some fraction as likely

636
00:24:58,640 --> 00:25:03,200
as the best one.

637
00:25:00,640 --> 00:25:05,520
Whatever survives is re-normalized into

638
00:25:03,200 --> 00:25:07,840
a distribution that sums to one, and a

639
00:25:05,520 --> 00:25:10,120
single token is drawn.

640
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:12,640
Not one weight has changed.

641
00:25:10,120 --> 00:25:15,120
The sampler only reshapes what the model

642
00:25:12,640 --> 00:25:17,080
already produced.

643
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:19,040
Sampling chooses among the tokens the

644
00:25:17,080 --> 00:25:20,880
model proposes.

645
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:22,720
Constraint decoding changes which tokens

646
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:24,560
are on offer in the first place, one

647
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:26,120
stage earlier.

648
00:25:24,560 --> 00:25:28,640
You need it whenever the output will be

649
00:25:26,120 --> 00:25:31,160
read by a machine rather than a person.

650
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:33,600
JSON that a parser will consume, a query

651
00:25:31,160 --> 00:25:36,920
that has to match a schema, a tool call

652
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:38,960
whose arguments have to type check, etc.

653
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:41,080
Asking for the format in the prompt is

654
00:25:38,960 --> 00:25:43,480
not a guarantee, because eventually the

655
00:25:41,080 --> 00:25:45,600
model emits one stray token and the

656
00:25:43,480 --> 00:25:48,960
parser fails.

657
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:51,280
So, the engine intervenes at the logits.

658
00:25:48,960 --> 00:25:53,400
Before the sampler runs, it sets the

659
00:25:51,280 --> 00:25:55,880
logit of every token that would break

660
00:25:53,400 --> 00:25:59,000
the format to negative infinity.

661
00:25:55,880 --> 00:26:01,000
Softmax turns those into exactly zero,

662
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:03,280
and a token with probability zero can

663
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:05,640
never be drawn, whatever sampler is

664
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:07,640
running above it.

665
00:26:05,640 --> 00:26:10,320
What decides which tokens would break

666
00:26:07,640 --> 00:26:12,640
the format is an automaton that tracks

667
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:14,120
the output so far.

668
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:16,400
For output shaped like a regular

669
00:26:14,120 --> 00:26:18,400
expression, a finite state machine is

670
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:21,560
enough, and that is the approach

671
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:23,480
outlines takes, reformulating generation

672
00:26:21,560 --> 00:26:25,160
as transitions between the states of a

673
00:26:23,480 --> 00:26:28,440
state machine.

674
00:26:25,160 --> 00:26:29,680
Grammars that nest, like JSON or SQL,

675
00:26:28,440 --> 00:26:31,480
need more,

676
00:26:29,680 --> 00:26:33,880
because a state machine cannot remember

677
00:26:31,480 --> 00:26:36,280
how many brackets are still open.

678
00:26:33,880 --> 00:26:38,760
That takes a pushdown automaton, a state

679
00:26:36,280 --> 00:26:40,680
machine plus a stack, which is what X

680
00:26:38,760 --> 00:26:43,440
grammar runs.

681
00:26:40,680 --> 00:26:46,600
The obvious worry is cost, because this

682
00:26:43,440 --> 00:26:50,160
runs at every single token.

683
00:26:46,600 --> 00:26:52,440
Done naively, you would test all 100,000

684
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:55,120
or more tokens in the vocabulary against

685
00:26:52,440 --> 00:26:57,800
the automaton at every step.

686
00:26:55,120 --> 00:27:00,560
Outlines removes that by pre-computing

687
00:26:57,800 --> 00:27:02,360
once per schema an index from each state

688
00:27:00,560 --> 00:27:04,760
of the machine to the set of tokens

689
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:08,560
allowed in it. So, the per step work

690
00:27:04,760 --> 00:27:11,680
becomes a look up constant on average.

691
00:27:08,560 --> 00:27:13,440
X grammar splits the vocabulary instead.

692
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:16,440
Most tokens can be checked ahead of

693
00:27:13,440 --> 00:27:19,040
time. Only a few need the live stack and

694
00:27:16,440 --> 00:27:20,840
the mask for those is built on the CPU

695
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:22,400
while the GPU is still running the

696
00:27:20,840 --> 00:27:24,440
forward pass.

697
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:26,920
Their masks land in under 40

698
00:27:24,440 --> 00:27:30,480
microseconds per token against [music] a

699
00:27:26,920 --> 00:27:32,320
decode step measured in milliseconds.

700
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:35,120
The guarantee this buys is exact and

701
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:36,960
narrow. The output will parse.

702
00:27:35,120 --> 00:27:37,920
Nothing about it is guaranteed to be

703
00:27:36,960 --> 00:27:39,440
true.

704
00:27:37,920 --> 00:27:42,400
And because the mask can remove the

705
00:27:39,440 --> 00:27:44,560
token the model most wanted, generation

706
00:27:42,400 --> 00:27:46,800
can be pushed down a path it would not

707
00:27:44,560 --> 00:27:48,800
otherwise have taken.

708
00:27:46,800 --> 00:27:50,680
And the mechanism generalizes [music]

709
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:52,240
past formatting.

710
00:27:50,680 --> 00:27:54,160
Restricting the next token to an

711
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:57,240
approved set [music] is how an agent's

712
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:59,560
actions are held inside a vetted schema.

713
00:27:57,240 --> 00:28:01,760
It cannot emit a call that is not on the

714
00:27:59,560 --> 00:28:03,880
list because those tokens are never

715
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:05,600
available to it.

716
00:28:03,880 --> 00:28:07,480
The serving layer is the last place

717
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:10,160
anything can be enforced before tokens

718
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:12,680
reach a user or a tool.

719
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:15,120
Step back and the whole stack is a chain

720
00:28:12,680 --> 00:28:16,760
of repairs.

721
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:19,680
Batching lets one read of the weights

722
00:28:16,760 --> 00:28:21,600
serve many requests at once.

723
00:28:19,680 --> 00:28:23,680
Static batches drain as the requests

724
00:28:21,600 --> 00:28:26,000
finish, so scheduling moved to one

725
00:28:23,680 --> 00:28:28,260
iteration at a time.

726
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:30,880
Attention never amortizes and KV caches

727
00:28:28,260 --> 00:28:33,920
[music] cap how many requests fit, so

728
00:28:30,880 --> 00:28:35,880
paging the cache made room for more.

729
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:37,280
Admitting a new request stalled everyone

730
00:28:35,880 --> 00:28:40,520
mid-generation.

731
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:42,200
So, prefills got sliced into chunks.

732
00:28:40,520 --> 00:28:43,880
Prefill and decode wanted different

733
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:45,960
hardware, so they were split into

734
00:28:43,880 --> 00:28:48,080
separate pools.

735
00:28:45,960 --> 00:28:49,880
Shared prompts were being recomputed, so

736
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:52,120
their keys and values got stored in a

737
00:28:49,880 --> 00:28:54,440
tree and reused.

738
00:28:52,120 --> 00:28:57,080
Around all of it, the scheduler defends

739
00:28:54,440 --> 00:28:59,160
good put by choosing what to refuse, and

740
00:28:57,080 --> 00:29:02,600
the sampler and the constraint machinery

741
00:28:59,160 --> 00:29:04,240
decide what actually comes out.

742
00:29:02,600 --> 00:29:05,680
Now, look at what every one of those has

743
00:29:04,240 --> 00:29:07,280
in common.

744
00:29:05,680 --> 00:29:09,640
Each raises the number of tokens

745
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:11,800
produced per read of the weights, and

746
00:29:09,640 --> 00:29:15,000
each does it the same way, by putting

747
00:29:11,800 --> 00:29:18,000
more requests behind that one read.

748
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:20,800
So, set the number of requests to one.

749
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:22,560
A single user, one conversation, nothing

750
00:29:20,800 --> 00:29:24,680
else on the machine.

751
00:29:22,560 --> 00:29:27,210
Every technique in this episode still

752
00:29:24,680 --> 00:29:27,480
runs, and not one of them helps.

753
00:29:27,210 --> 00:29:29,800
>> [music]

754
00:29:27,480 --> 00:29:32,880
>> That stream pays a full weight read for

755
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:35,600
every single token it produces, exactly

756
00:29:32,880 --> 00:29:38,560
as it did at the start of this episode.

757
00:29:35,600 --> 00:29:40,200
It is as memory-bound as it ever was,

758
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:42,400
because there is nothing to share the

759
00:29:40,200 --> 00:29:44,840
read with.

760
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:46,840
There is one direction left. If you

761
00:29:44,840 --> 00:29:49,200
cannot put more requests behind a weight

762
00:29:46,840 --> 00:29:51,480
read, the only thing left is to get more

763
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:53,120
than one token out of it for the same

764
00:29:51,480 --> 00:29:55,320
request.

765
00:29:53,120 --> 00:29:59,040
That is speculative decoding, and it is

766
00:29:55,320 --> 00:29:59,040
where the next episode starts.

